==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{m|gem-pro|*haitaną||command, name}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*kei-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*ki-||put in motion, be moving}}. Cognate with {{etyl|ofs|-}} {{m|ofs|hēta}}, {{etyl|osx|-}} {{m|osx|hētan}}, {{etyl|goh|-}} {{m|goh|heizzan}}, {{etyl|non|-}} {{m|non|heita}} ({{etyl|sv|-}} {{m|sv|heta}}), {{etyl|got|-}} {{m|got|𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽}}. The Indo-European root is also the source of {{etyl|grc|-}} {{m|grc|κίειν||put in motion}}, {{etyl|la|-}} {{m|la|ciere||rouse, make active}} and {{etyl|sq|-}} {{m|sq|cys||to spur, set in motion}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈhɑːtɑn/|lang=ang}}

